A chart type lands in `dev` with its schema, `learn` entry, a golden input with sourced data, edge fixtures (empty, single value, negative, missing, extremes, dense) and unit tests of its geometry and checks, so the fast gate stays quick (DEL-81); agents add browser or visual runs when they judge them useful. It is accepted into `pre-prod`, and `learn`, the docs and every count list it, only when the release run (DEL-81) shows all of these for it (DEL-18):

- its row in FEA-CH-69's table, with a test that each listed check fires;
- geometry checks: no clipping or collision at 320, 390 and 1440 px in every style;
- values at the author's precision in readouts, Table, CSV and exports (DEL-52, FEA-CH-37);
- its interaction policy (FEA-CH-39) with pointer and keyboard tests;
- its motion spec with motion tests (DEL-75);
- its accessible summary (FEA-CH-55);
- vector output in a PDF pagination fixture (FEA-RP-43);
- captures: one case per type, per style, at 390 and 1440 px (DEL-27);
- a local comparison against the libraries that define the type (ggplot2, Plotly, ECharts, Highcharts, Power BI) on the same data (ARC-64), shown on its first design card and judged against Daniel's INT-12 bar, charts that withstand side-by-side comparison with Highcharts and ECharts;
- an entry in the all-types performance fixture (PRF-07).

The type list compiled from those libraries arrives as feature cards per family, so each type is accepted before it is built.

Limits: a type that needs a size limit (nodes, links, cells, grid points) declares it in its schema and `learn` entry, and the value is set so the dense fixture stays within PRF-32. Above a limit, `check` reports an error diagnostic that names the limit and the count, so the author filters or aggregates. A limit never drops or truncates data silently, and totals, shares and readouts always reflect all the data. Raising a limit beyond PRF-32 needs a card that states the cost (PRF-05).
